/// A label somebody attaches to rows of several different models.
///
/// ─── Read this before copying it ──────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **There is no instance of this in the tree.** `AuditEvent` and `Notification`
/// are derived from working models; this one is argued from a production Prisma
/// schema outside the repo plus the one precedent below, so it is the file most
/// likely to be wrong. Treat it as a starting position, not a finding.
///
/// ─── The hard part, which is not the tag ──────────────────────────────────
///
/// A tag is four columns and no argument. The ATTACHMENT is the whole problem:
/// one tag goes on rows of many models, and a foreign key names a table, not a
/// union. There is no polymorphic relation in this framework and adding one is
/// not a small feature — it is the same wall `IDEAS/schema-variants.md` hits
/// from the other side.
///
/// Three honest answers and none is free:
///
///   1. A JOIN TABLE PER TAGGABLE MODEL — `ProductTag`, `OrderTag`, `ContactTag`.
///      Real foreign keys, so the database refuses a dangling attachment and
///      `onDelete: Cascade` cleans up by itself. Costs one table per taggable
///      model and a union query for *everything tagged `urgent`*.
///
///   2. ONE TABLE WITH AN EXCLUSIVE ARC — one `TagAttachment` carrying a nullable
///      foreign key per taggable model and `@@arc([orderId, productId, …])`, which
///      is a table CHECK saying exactly one is set. Keeps every foreign key and
///      every cascade that 1 has, in one table, so *everything tagged `urgent`*
///      is one query. Costs one COLUMN per taggable model rather than one table,
///      and stops scaling somewhere around six — which is the signal that the set
///      is open and you are in case 3.
///
///   3. ONE TABLE WITH A POLYMORPHIC SUBJECT — `subjectType` + `subjectId`, the
///      shape `AuditEvent` already uses in this tree. One table, one query for
///      the cross-model read, and NO referential integrity: nothing stops a row
///      naming a model that does not exist or an id that has been deleted, and
///      every read is a join the database cannot check.
///
/// **Reach for 2 first** whenever the taggable set is closed and small — it is
/// the only one of the three that keeps integrity AND the cross-model query, and
/// it is what `@@arc` was added for. **1** is worth it when the join rows need
/// columns of their own that differ per model. **3 only when the set is open** —
/// tagging as a platform feature, or a taggable list that grows without a schema
/// change. The sketch below is 3, because that is the case a reference is useful
/// for and the one whose costs need stating; 2 is written in `docs/schema.md`
/// § *Exclusive foreign keys*, and 1 needs no reference, being ordinary join
/// tables.
///
/// If you take 3, own the cost explicitly: something has to sweep attachments
/// whose subject is gone, and *something* means a job, because the database will
/// not. That sweep is the whole of what 1 and 2 get for free.
///
/// ─── What is deliberately NOT here ────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// **No `count`.** A denormalised total goes stale on every attach and detach
/// and there is no trigger to keep it. `@from(TagAttachment, count: true)` is a
/// correlated subquery and is always right.
///
/// **No `parent` for tag groups.** A hierarchy is a second feature with its own
/// questions (does a child inherit the parent's attachments? does filtering by a
/// parent match children?) and neither has an obvious answer. `group` below is a
/// flat string, which covers "these tags are colours and those are statuses"
/// without pretending to be a tree.
model Tag {
  id        Int      @id

  /// The tenant, where there is one. A plain column, not a relation — this file
  /// declares no tenant model. Wire it in your own schema.
  ///
  /// It is REQUIRED here, unlike `AuditEvent`'s: a tag with no owner is one
  /// every tenant reads and any tenant can attach.
  ownerId   String

  name      String   @trim @length(1, 40) @label("Tag")

  /// Flat, not a tree. "colour", "status", "priority" — what KIND of tag this
  /// is, so a picker can offer one kind and a filter can ask for one.
  group     String?  @length(1, 40)

  /// A token name, never a hex value — Invariant 13. `danger`, `info`, `muted`.
  /// A stored `#ff0000` is a tag that ignores the theme and is unreadable in
  /// half of them.
  color     String?  @length(1, 30)

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// Unique per OWNER and per group, not globally: two tenants both having
  /// `urgent` is correct, and so is a `red` colour tag beside a `red` status.
  ///
  /// Note what this cannot say if `group` is nullable — two NULLs never compare
  /// equal in SQL, so `(1, 'urgent', NULL)` would be admissible twice while the
  /// same pair with a group is refused. Litestone refuses a composite `@@unique`
  /// over a nullable column at parse for exactly that reason; state
  /// `nullsDistinct: true` if you mean it, or make `group` required.
  @@unique([ownerId, name, group], nullsDistinct: true)

  /// Read 4 · create 4 · update 4 · delete 5.
  ///
  /// Anyone signed in reads and creates a tag; deleting one takes it off every
  /// row it is attached to, which is why delete is a level higher.
  @@gate("4.4.4.5")
}

/// The attachment. One row per (tag, thing).
///
/// Separate from `Tag` and not a `Json` array of ids on either side, for the
/// reason every such array eventually teaches: an array cannot be indexed,
/// joined, or counted, and litestone classifies a `Json` column as opaque — so
/// it cannot be sorted or grouped by either.
model TagAttachment {
  id          Int      @id

  /// Wire the relation to `Tag` in your own schema, where both models exist:
  ///
  ///   tag Tag @relation(fields: [tagId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  ///
  /// `Cascade` is the right action and is what makes deleting a tag a single
  /// statement rather than a job.
  tagId       Int

  /// The polymorphic subject — same spelling as `AuditEvent`, which is the
  /// catalogue's preference for anything new. `String` for the id because it
  /// must hold whatever the taggable models use, and `Int` cannot hold a uuid
  /// while `String` holds both.
  ///
  /// **Nothing enforces this pair.** That is the cost of answer 3 above, stated
  /// where somebody copying the file will read it — and the reason to check
  /// whether `@@arc` covers your taggable set before taking it.
  subjectType String
  subjectId   String

  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())

  /// One tag on one thing once. This is the constraint that makes attaching
  /// idempotent, which every tag UI needs and few remember to ask for.
  @@unique([tagId, subjectType, subjectId])

  /// Two reads, two directions: this row's tags, and everything tagged X.
  @@index([subjectType, subjectId])
  @@index([tagId])

  /// Read 4 · create 4 · update 9 · delete 4.
  ///
  /// Update is LOCKED because there is nothing on this row to edit — moving an
  /// attachment is a delete and a create, and allowing an update means allowing
  /// a row to be re-pointed at a subject the caller may not be able to read.
  @@gate("4.4.9.4")
}
